'''Gideon Welles''' (July 1, 1802 – February 11, 1878) was an American government official who was the [[United States Secretary of the Navy]] from 1861 to 1869, a cabinet post he was awarded after supporting [[Abraham Lincoln]] in the [[1860 United States presidential election|1860 election]]. Although opposed to the [[Union blockade]] of Southern ports, he duly carried out his part of the [[Anaconda Plan]], largely sealing off the Confederate coastline and preventing the exchange of cotton for war supplies. This is viewed as a major cause of Union victory in the [[American Civil War|Civil War]], and his achievement in expanding the Navy almost tenfold was widely praised. Welles was also instrumental in the Navy's creation of the [[Medal of Honor]]. Lincoln nicknamed him his "Neptune".

==Early life and marriage==
Gideon Welles, the son of Samuel Welles and Ann Hale,{{sfn |Hale |1909|p=[https://archive.org/details/ancestrydescenda00hale/page/112/mode/2up 113]}} was born on July 1, 1802, in [[Glastonbury, Connecticut]].<ref name="NYTobit"/>
His father was a shipping merchant and fervent [[Jeffersonian democracy|Jeffersonian]];{{sfn |Niven |1973 |p=6}} he was a member of the Convention, which formed the first state [[Connecticut Constitution]] in 1818 that abolished the colonial charter and officially severed the pre-[[American Revolution]] political ties to England. In contrast to the [[Fundamental Orders of Connecticut]], the successor constitution of 1818 provided for freedom of religion.  He was a member of the seventh generation of his family in America. His original immigrant ancestor was [[Thomas Welles]],{{sfn |Norton |1905|pp=19–21}}{{sfn |Niven |1973 |p=7}} who arrived in 1635 and was the only man in Connecticut's history to hold all four top offices: governor, deputy governor, treasurer, and secretary. He was also the transcriber of the Fundamental Orders. Welles was the second great-grandson of Capt. Samuel Welles and Ruth (Rice) Welles, the daughter of [[Edmund Rice (1638)|Edmund Rice]], a 1638 immigrant to [[Sudbury, Massachusetts|Sudbury]] and founder of [[Marlborough, Massachusetts]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.edmund-rice.org/era5gens/p37.htm#i350937 | title=Gideon Welles in ERA database | publisher=[[Edmund Rice (1638)]] Association, Inc. | access-date=March 15, 2011 | archive-date=July 25, 2011 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110725010813/http://www.edmund-rice.org/era5gens/p37.htm#i350937 | url-status=dead }}</ref>

He married on June 16, 1835, at Lewiston, Mifflin County, Pennsylvania, [[Mary Jane Hale Welles|Mary Jane Hale]],<ref name="NYTobit1">{{cite web
  | title = Death of Mrs. Gideon Welles
  | work = [[The New York Times]]
  | date = March 4, 1886
  | url = https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1886/03/04/106181617.pdf
  | page = 4
  | access-date = 2025-10-23}}</ref> who was born on June 18, 1817, in Glastonbury, Connecticut, the daughter of Elias White Hale and Jane Mullhallan.  Her father, Elias, graduated from [[Yale College]] in 1794 and practiced law in Mifflin and Centre Counties, Pennsylvania.{{sfn |Niven |1973 |p=16}} She died on February 28, 1886, in Hartford, Connecticut, and was buried next to her husband in Cedar Hill Cemetery in Hartford. Gideon and Mary Jane were the parents of six children.

He was educated at the [[Cheshire Academy|Episcopal Academy at Cheshire, Connecticut]], and earned a degree at the American Literary, Scientific, and Military Academy at Norwich, Vermont  (later [[Norwich University]]).<ref name="NYTobit"/> He became a lawyer through the then-common practice of reading the law, but soon shifted to journalism and became the founder and editor of the ''[[Hartford Times]]'' in 1826. 

==Early political career==
After successfully gaining admission, from 1827 to 1835, he participated in the [[Connecticut House of Representatives]] as a Democrat. Following his service in the [[Connecticut General Assembly]], he served in various posts, including State Controller of Public Accounts in 1835, [[Postmaster]] of [[Hartford, Connecticut|Hartford]] (1836–41), and Chief of the [[United States Navy bureau system|Bureau of Provisions and Clothing]] for the Navy (1846–49).<ref name="Gideon Welles papers, 1777-1911">{{cite web |title=Gideon Welles papers, 1777–1911 |website=Library of Congress Finding Aid |url=https://hdl.loc.gov/loc.mss/eadmss.ms003053}}</ref>

Welles was a [[Jacksonian democracy|Jacksonian Democrat]] who worked very closely with [[Martin Van Buren]] and [[John Milton Niles]]. His chief rival in the Connecticut Democratic Party was [[Isaac Toucey]], whom Welles would later replace at the Navy Department. While Welles dutifully supported [[James K. Polk]] in the 1844 election, he would abandon the Democrats in 1848 to support Van Buren's [[Free Soil Party|Free Soil]] campaign.{{sfn |Earle |2004 |pp=73–75}}

Mainly because of his strong [[abolitionism in the United States|anti-slavery]] views, Welles shifted allegiance in 1854 to the newly established [[United States Republican Party|Republican Party]] and founded a newspaper in 1856 (the ''Hartford Evening Press'') that would espouse Republican ideals for decades thereafter.  He was the Republican nominee for governor in the [[1856 Connecticut gubernatorial election]], coming in third behind [[William T. Minor]] and [[Samuel Ingham]].

