[[File:Running the "machine" LCCN2003689257.jpg|thumb|''The Running Machine'':<br /> An 1864 cartoon takes a swing at the Lincoln administration.    It features Welles, [[William P. Fessenden]], [[Edwin M. Stanton]], [[Abraham Lincoln]] and [[William H. Seward]].]]
Welles' strong support of [[Abraham Lincoln]] in the [[1860 United States presidential election|1860 presidential election]] made him the logical candidate from New England for Lincoln's cabinet. In March 1861, Lincoln named Welles his [[United States Secretary of the Navy|Secretary of the Navy]].

Welles found the [[United States Department of the Navy|Naval Department]] in disarray, with Southern officers resigning en masse. His first major action was to dispatch the Navy's most powerful warship, the [[USS Powhatan (1850)|USS ''Powhatan'']], to relieve Fort Sumter on Lincoln's instructions. Unfortunately, Secretary of State [[William H. Seward|Seward]] had just ordered the ''Powhatan'' to [[Fort Pickens]], Florida on his own authority, ruining whatever chance Major [[Robert Anderson (Union officer)|Robert Anderson]] had of withstanding the assault. Several weeks later, when Seward argued for a blockade of Southern ports, Welles argued vociferously against the action but was eventually overruled by Lincoln. Despite his misgivings, Welles' efforts to rebuild the Navy and implement the blockade proved extraordinarily effective. From 76 ships and 7,600 sailors in 1861, the Navy expanded almost tenfold by 1865. His implementation of the Naval portion of the [[Anaconda Plan]] strongly weakened the Confederacy's ability to finance the war by limiting the cotton trade, and while never completely effective in sealing off all 3,500 miles of Southern coastline, it was a major contribution towards Northern victory. Lincoln nicknamed Welles his "[[Neptune (mythology)|Neptune]]."<ref name="Marinaro, Michael.">{{cite web|url=http://connecticuthistory.org/gideon-welles-us-secretary-of-the-navy-and-lincolns-neptune/|title=Gideon Welles, US Secretary of the Navy and Lincoln's 'Neptune' |author=Marinaro, Michael. |website=Connecticut History |access-date= April 9, 2016}}
</ref>

After Lincoln's assassination, Welles was retained by President [[Andrew Johnson]] as Secretary of the Navy. In 1866, Welles, along with Seward, was instrumental in launching the National Union Party as a third party alternative supportive of Johnson's reconciliation policies. Welles also played a prominent part in Johnson's ill-fated "[[Swing Around the Circle]]" campaign that autumn.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=DOC3SMl00cgC&pg=PA230 |title=Lincoln's Avengers: Justice, Revenge, and Reunion After the Civil War |first=Elizabeth D. |last=Leonard |author-link=Elizabeth D. Leonard |publisher=[[W. W. Norton & Company]] |isbn=9780393048681 |page=230 |date=2004 |access-date=2025-10-23 |via=Google Books}}</ref> Although Welles admitted in his diary that he was dismayed by Johnson's behavior on the trip, particularly the president's penchant for invective and engaging directly with hecklers, Welles remained loyal to Johnson, and supported him during [[Impeachment of Andrew Johnson|his impeachment]].<ref name="Marinaro, Michael."/> On April 17 and 18, 1868, Welles testified in [[Impeachment trial of Andrew Johnson|the impeachment trial of Johnson]], having been called as a witness by Johnson's defense team.<ref>{{cite book |title=Extracts from the Journal of the United States Senate In All Cases of Impeachment Presented By The United States House of Representatives (1798–1904) |series=Congressional serial set |pages=278–281 |date=1912 |publisher=Washington Government Printing Office |url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc1.$b628530&view=1up&seq=286}}</ref> His son Edgar also testified that day.<ref>{{cite news |title=By Telegraph Saturday's Dispatches From Washington |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-st-louis-republic-by-telegraph-satur/183532121/ |newspaper=Daily [[Missouri Republican]] |publication-place=St. Louis |page=1 |via=Newspapers.com |access-date=2025-10-23 |language=en |date=April 20, 1980}}</ref>

==Later life and death==
[[File:GideonWellesPortrait.jpg|thumb|upright|Gideon Welles]]
After leaving politics, Welles returned to Connecticut and to writing, editing his journals, and authoring several books before his death, including a biography, ''Lincoln and Seward'', published in 1874.<ref name="NYTobit"/>

He was a Third Class Companion of the [[Military Order of the Loyal Legion of the United States]].  While the Loyal Legion did consist predominantly of Union officers who had served in the [[American Civil War]] the Order's constitution provided for honorary members (i.e. Third Class Companions) who were civilians who had made significant contributions to the war effort. Welles was also instrumental in the Navy's creation of the [[Medal of Honor]].<ref name="MOH">{{cite web|title=The Navy's Medal of Honor|publisher=Department of the Navy – Naval Historical Center|date=October 30, 2007|url=http://www.history.navy.mil/faqs/faq38-1.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19970709142530/http://www.history.navy.mil/faqs/faq38-1.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=July 9, 1997|access-date=August 21, 2010|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

Towards the end of 1877, his health began to wane. Welles died from a streptococcal throat infection, at the age of seventy-five, on February 11, 1878.<ref name="NYTobit">{{cite web
  | title = Obituary: Hon. Gideon Welles
  | work = [[The New York Times]]
  | date = February 12, 1878
  | url = https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1878/02/12/80675443.pdf
  | page = 6
  | access-date = 2025-10-23}}</ref> His body was interred at [[Cedar Hill Cemetery (Hartford, Connecticut)|Cedar Hill Cemetery]] in [[Hartford, Connecticut]].

==Legacy==
Two ships have been named [[USS Welles|USS ''Welles'']] in his honor. The Dining Commons at [[Cheshire Academy]] and the Gideon Welles School in [[Glastonbury, Connecticut]], are also named after him.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.cheshireacademy.org/page/student-life/campus-resources/gideon-welles-dining-hall|title=Gideon Welles Dining Hall|website=www.cheshireacademy.org|publisher=Cheshire Academy|language=en|access-date=2017-01-24|archive-date=February 2, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170202032817/http://www.cheshireacademy.org/page/student-life/campus-resources/gideon-welles-dining-hall|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://sites.google.com/a/glastonburyus.org/gideon/|title=Gideon Welles School|website=sites.google.com|access-date=2017-01-24}}</ref> In the [[Lincoln Square, Chicago|Lincoln Square]] neighborhood of Chicago, [[Welles Park (Chicago)|Welles Park]] was dedicated in his honor in 1910,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.chicagoparkdistrict.com/parks-facilities/Welles-Park/|title=Welles (Gideon) Park &#124; Chicago Park District|website=www.chicagoparkdistrict.com}}</ref> and an adjacent restaurant, opened in 2014, was also named after Gideon Welles.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.gideonchicago.com|title=Home {{!}} Gideon Welles in Chicago, IL {{!}} Food & Drink|website=Gideon Welles|language=en-US|access-date=2018-03-28}}</ref>

He was not an ancestor of [[Orson Welles]] as the actor had claimed on ''[[The Dick Cavett Show]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://newenglandhistoricalsociety.com/gideon-welles-civil-war-diplomacy-gossip-band-concerts/|title=Gideon Welles, June 8, 1863: Civil War, Diplomacy, Mrs. Lincoln and Weekly Marine Band Concerts|first=Leslie|last=Landrigan|date=June 8, 2014|website=New England Historical Society}}</ref>

